3. tourism-crime-corolation -
    This analysis investigates whether crime patterns near major tourist attractions in New York City differ from those across the city.
Using GeoPandas, 500-meter buffer zones are created around tourist attractions in a projected coordinate system (EPSG:32618), and NYPD
crime records are spatially joined to classify incidents as occurring inside or outside tourist zones. Crime density is compared between
these areas, while a Lift metric is used to identify specific offense types that are disproportionately common near tourist attractions.
A chi-squared test evaluates whether the overall crime-type distribution differs significantly between tourist and non-tourist areas,
and a sensitivity analysis using 250m, 500m, and 1000m buffers tests the robustness of the findings across different spatial scales.
